var tipuesearch = {"pages":[{"title":" fpm-watch ","text":"fpm-watch fpm-watch fpm-watch reruns fpm build , fpm test , or fpm run when relevant files\nchange. It uses fpm's dependency model to select files and affected targets.\nPolling uses OS sleep between scans; dependency watching and a restart supervisor\nare optional. Installation Use fpm 0.13.0, a Fortran compiler, and a C compiler. The fpm library dependency is\npinned to 0.13.0 in fpm.toml . git clone https://github.com/gha3mi/fpm-watch.git cd fpm-watch\nfpm install --profile release Put both fpm and the installed fpm-watch executable on PATH . Usage fpm-watch [watch options] <build|test|run> [fpm arguments...] Run from an fpm project, or select one with fpm's --directory / -C option: fpm-watch build\nfpm-watch test fpm-watch run first -- input.dat\nfpm-watch --watch-deps run --example my_example\nfpm-watch run --directory path/to/project Place watcher options before the subcommand. Everything after it is forwarded\nto fpm; use fpm's -- delimiter for application arguments. Explicit target names, --target first,second , and --all retain fpm's selection. Watching and target selection The command runs at startup by default. After that, the watcher polls known paths,\nwaits for the debounce interval after detecting an edit, and checks again.\nCommands run synchronously: polling pauses while fpm runs, and subsequent scans\ndetect edits made during that run. The watch list contains relevant sources, nested Fortran/CPP includes, quoted C\nheaders, manifests, manifest version files, and response-file lookup candidates.\nBuild mode watches project sources from fpm's model. Run/test mode watches the\nselected targets' dependency closures. --watch-deps includes dependency projects. For unpinned run/test commands with 1–64 targets, source edits normally run only\naffected targets. The original command runs when metadata changes, target/path\nmembership changes, or affected-target mapping is unavailable. Explicit user\nselections remain part of that original command. Source/include edits refresh the graph before execution. If that refresh fails,\nthe previous watch list is retained and the original command runs so fpm can\nreport the failure. Manifest/response edits also reparse settings; if that\nconfiguration refresh fails, execution waits until a later edit or rescan succeeds. New sources are discovered during graph refreshes. Use fpm-watch --watch-rescan 2 test to discover additions without another watched\npath changing. Rescans are disabled by default. Small poll intervals and frequent\nrescans increase work; each polling scan hashes complete file contents. Stopping and supervision Press Ctrl+C to stop the watcher and interrupt an active command. Alternatively,\ncreate .fpm-watch.stop in the selected project directory. The stop file is checked\nbetween commands; it does not interrupt an active command. Remove it before the\nnext session. Startup execution still occurs if run-on-start is enabled. --watch-auto-restart restarts a watcher after a nonzero exit. Continuous watching\ncontinues after fpm failures. In one-shot mode, fpm failure becomes the watcher's\nexit status and can trigger a restart. User interruption ends supervision. Response files Use a named options-only fpm response file: # dev.rsp\noptions run\noptions --profile release Run it with fpm-watch run @dev . @dev searches for dev.rsp in the current\ndirectory and CLI_RESPONSE_PATH . Use @./config/dev.rsp for an exact path.\nOptions, quoting, and named-file OS sections follow fpm's parser. Include an options build , options test , or options run record; it may override the\ncommand-line subcommand. Comments and OS section headers are allowed. Response files and lookup candidates are watched, including missing candidates\nwhose creation could change lookup. Procedural records such as system / print / stop and executable-specific compound response files are\nunsupported. When response parsing is active, other raw @ arguments, even\nafter -- , must also resolve to options-only response files. fpm cache limitation In fpm 0.13.0, some include-only edits can leave cached objects unchanged even\nthough the watcher reruns the command. For the default build directory, fpm clean --skip forces recompilation on the next build while retaining\ndownloaded dependencies. Watch options Value options accept --watch-option VALUE or --watch-option=VALUE .\nOptional boolean and verbosity values require = : a bare boolean flag enables\nthe setting, and =false disables it. All times are seconds. Option Effect --help , -h , --watch-help Show help and exit. --watch-deps[=BOOL] , --watch-no-deps Enable or disable dependency watching. --watch-poll SEC Poll interval; minimum 0.05. --watch-debounce SEC Delay after detecting an edit before resampling. --watch-rescan SEC , --watch-no-rescan Periodic graph refresh; 0 disables it. --watch-run-on-start , --watch-no-run-on-start Enable or disable startup execution. --watch-once[=BOOL] Initialize and optionally run once; propagate fpm's status, or exit 0 after successful initialization without a run. --watch-include GLOB , --watch-ignore GLOB Repeatable include/ignore filters. --watch-fpm PATH fpm executable path or name. --watch-silent-fpm[=BOOL] Suppress both command output streams. --watch-log-format FORMAT text , json , or logfmt . --watch-quiet , -q Watcher errors only, except forced output-marker events. --watch-verbose[=N] , -v Raise verbosity to N; default 1, maximum 2. --watch-very-verbose , -vv Raise verbosity to 2; list paths after refreshes. --watch-print-files[=BOOL] List up to 200 startup paths; enabling the CLI flag raises verbosity to 2. --watch-debug[=BOOL] Enable debug records unless quiet mode suppresses them. --watch-output-markers[=BOOL] Add command-output markers in structured logging modes. --watch-auto-restart[=BOOL] Enable or disable supervision. --watch-restart-delay SEC Delay before a supervisor restart. --watch-restart-max N Maximum restarts after the initial launch; 0 means unlimited. --watch-self PATH Watcher executable used by the supervisor; normally auto-detected. Quote glob arguments against shell expansion. Patterns match watched paths\nnormalized to / separators; use --watch-print-files to inspect them. An empty\ninclude list admits all eligible paths; otherwise, at least one include pattern\nmust match. Ignore matches exclude paths. Manifests, version metadata, and\nresponse-file candidates bypass these filters. Configuration Add [extra.fpm-watch] to the selected project's fpm.toml . The following values\nare the built-in defaults: [extra.fpm-watch] poll = 0.5 debounce = 0.2 rescan = 0.0 run-on-start = true once = false deps = false silent-fpm = false print-files = false debug = false verbosity = 0 log-format = \"text\" fpm-executable = \"fpm\" output-markers = false auto-restart = false restart-delay = 1.0 restart-max = 0 self = \"\" ignore = [] include = [] Leading CLI flags override manifest settings. Include/ignore flags append unique\nentries, and verbosity flags raise rather than replace the current level. verbosity=-1 selects quiet mode. For a startup path list configured only in the\nmanifest, set both print-files=true and verbosity=2 . Negative debounce, rescan, and restart delays become 0. The poll interval is\nclamped to at least 0.05. The log format stays fixed across configuration refreshes\nand supervisor restarts; restart the whole watcher session to change it. Logging and integrations Text logs have timestamps and no ANSI colors; errors use stderr. Quiet mode\ncontrols watcher records, while --watch-silent-fpm controls command output. In JSON Lines or logfmt mode, watcher records, including errors, use stdout.\nRaw fpm/model output and compiler/runtime diagnostics use stderr. Keep the streams\nseparate: fpm-watch --watch-log-format = json build > watch.jsonl 2 > fpm.log\nfpm-watch --watch-log-format = logfmt test > watch.log 2 > fpm.log Records contain timestamp (RFC 3339), level , event , and message (JSON) or msg (logfmt), and are flushed immediately. JSON attributes retain their types;\nlogfmt arrays are quoted JSON. Correlate command.started and command.finished with cycle_id . Completion\nincludes exit_code , duration_ms , and interrupted . Treat IDs as opaque and\nscope them to one watcher session. At verbosity 1, watch.configuration reports\neffective settings at startup and after successful refreshes. To frame each command's raw output, enable markers: fpm-watch --watch-log-format = json --watch-output-markers build Stderr receives @@fpm-watch:started:ID@@ and @@fpm-watch:finished:ID@@ around command output. The ID matches cycle_id . With markers enabled, both\ncommand events remain visible even in quiet mode. Stdout and stderr delivery order\ncan differ; integrations should correlate by ID. Bash completion From this repository, install and load the completion script: mkdir -p ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions\ncp completion.bash ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/fpm-watch source ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/fpm-watch Development fpm build --profile debug --verbose\nfpm build --profile release --verbose\nfpm test fpm install --prefix .\n./bin/fpm-watch --watch-once test --profile debug\npython test/integration.py --watcher ./bin/fpm-watch\npython test/response_regression.py --watcher ./bin/fpm-watch\npython test/platform_regression.py ./bin/fpm-watch\npython test/release_regression.py On Windows, use .\\bin\\fpm-watch.exe for direct execution and --watcher ./bin/fpm-watch.exe for the Python suites that accept --watcher .\nThe platform suite takes the executable as a positional argument. The checks need\nPython 3.9+ and use its standard library and temporary projects. Release tests require\nBash and jq and are skipped on Windows. Optional local checks: fortitude check\nford README.md Documentation uses FORD only and is written to doc/ . CI builds with gfortran\non Linux/macOS/Windows and ifx on Linux. The macOS job uses the GNU ci-release profile ( -O1 ); other release jobs use release , and tests use debug . Release helper The package reads its version from VERSION . release.sh uses the previous\nrelease tag as its baseline, or VERSION when there are no tags. Patch changes\npromote a prerelease to its stable core ( v1.2.3-rc.1 → v1.2.3 ); feature\nand breaking changes advance the minor and major versions. Invalid baseline\nversions fail before files are modified. CI status This block is maintained by CI. OS Compiler Version fpm ubuntu 24.04 gfortran 16.2.0 0.13.0 ✅ ubuntu 24.04 ifx 2026.1.1 0.13.0 ✅ macos 26 gfortran 16.2.0 0.13.0 ✅ windows 2025 gfortran 16.2.0 0.13.0 ✅ Developer Info Seyed Ali Ghasemi","tags":"home","url":"index.html"},{"title":"log_field_t – fpm-watch ","text":"type, public :: log_field_t A typed event attribute, stored as its JSON representation. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial character(len=:), public, allocatable :: key Attribute name ( key ) and its complete serialized JSON representation ( value ). character(len=:), public, allocatable :: value Attribute name ( key ) and its complete serialized JSON representation ( value ). Source Code type log_field_t !> Attribute name (`key`) and its complete serialized JSON representation (`value`). character ( len = :), allocatable :: key , value end type log_field_t","tags":"","url":"type/log_field_t.html"},{"title":"file_collection_t – fpm-watch ","text":"type, private :: file_collection_t Shared growable path/mask collection with indexed, cached include edges. Inherits type~~file_collection_t~~InheritsGraph type~file_collection_t file_collection_t string_t string_t type~file_collection_t->string_t paths type~include_edges_t include_edges_t type~file_collection_t->type~include_edges_t includes Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Type Type This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a derived type to the parent